{"id":"c0e848f8-02c9-4aef-9b8e-3f65b7fa775f","object":"chat.completion","created":1784980300,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be a dict\\\")\\n    if \\\"threshold\\\" not in case or \\\"calls\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"missing threshold or calls\\\")\\n    threshold = case[\\\"threshold\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"threshold must be numeric\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"calls must be a list\\\")\\n    diag_list = []\\n    accepted_ids = []\\n    for i, call in enumerate(calls):\\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"call {i} is not a dict\\\")\\n        required = [\\\"call_id\\\", \\\"loss_without_call\\\", \\\"loss_with_call\\\", \\\"prediction_tokens\\\", \\\"parseable\\\"]\\n        for key in required:\\n            if key not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"call {i} missing {key}\\\")\\n        call_id = call[\\\"call_id\\\"]\\n        loss_without = call[\\\"loss_without_call\\\"]\\n        loss_with = call[\\\"loss_with_call\\\"]\\n        pred_tokens = call[\\\"prediction_tokens\\\"]\\n        parseable = call[\\\"parseable\\\"]\\n        if not isinstance(call_id, str):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"call {i} call_id not string\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)) or not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"call {i} loss values must be numeric\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(pred_tokens, int) or pred_tokens < 1:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"call {i} prediction_tokens must be positive integer\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"call {i} parseable must be boolean\\\")\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        normalized_gain = gain / pred_tokens\\n        accepted = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold\\n        gain_rounded = round(gain, 8)\\n        normalized_rounded = round(normalized_gain, 8)\\n        diag = {\\n            \\\"call_id\\\": call_id,\\n            \\\"gain\\\": gain_rounded,\\n            \\\"normalized_gain\\\": normalized_rounded,\\n            \\\"accepted\\\": accepted\\n        }\\n        diag_list.append(diag)\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_ids.append(call_id)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"accepted_call_ids\\\": accepted_ids,\\n        \\\"diagnostics\\\": diag_list\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce Python source code for solve(case) that implements the described logic. The payload has \"threshold\" and \"calls\". Each call has call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable. The task: For each call, compute gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call. Normalize gain by at least one prediction token? Actually \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" Means divide gain by prediction_tokens, but if prediction_tokens is 0? Should use max(1, prediction_tokens). Or maybe ensure denominator at least 1. The instruction: \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" So normalize gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / max(1, prediction_tokens). Then require both parseable == True and normalized gain >= threshold (inclusive threshold). Keep diagnostics in supplied call order. Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics. The diagnostics per call? Likely return a dictionary with \"accepted_call_ids\" (list of call_ids in original order of calls that pass), and maybe \"diagnostics\" list of dicts per call? The description: \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" And \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\" So we should return an object with keys \"accepted_call_ids\" and \"diagnostics\" (list of dicts for each call? Or maybe just for accepted? Usually per-call diagnostics includes for all calls. But \"rounded\" means round numbers? The specification: \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". We need to decide what fields to include. The instruction says: \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" And \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\". Likely include call_id, gain, normalized_gain, parseable, threshold met? But the phrase \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" suggests we output something like for each call: call_id, normalized_gain (rounded), maybe gain? The objective: \"Retain API-call candidates using normalized future-token weights, weighted losses, the stronger counterfactual, and an inclusive threshold.\" That seems like the filtering rule described. The result should be a JSON-serializable object. The typical pattern: return {\"accepted_call_ids\": [...], \"diagnostics\": [{\"call_id\": ..., \"gain\": ..., \"normalized_gain\": ..., \"parseable\": ..., \"accepted\": ...}...] }. But the problem says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". Probably round to a reasonable number of decimal places? Maybe to 8 decimal places? Or just round to 4? Not specified. Could use round(x, 8). But we can also just round to 10 digits. The example in the fixtures doesn't show expected output. However, the user said \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So we need to define fields. The description: \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" So perhaps the diagnostics list contains for each call (all calls in order): call_id, normalized_gain (rounded), and accepted (boolean). Or \"gain\" (raw). But \"normalized_weights\" is mentioned as property, but not used. Actually \"normalized_weights\" might refer to the normalized gain? The phrase \"weighted losses\" and \"stronger counterfactual\" maybe means something else? Wait: \"Toolformer API-call filtering with supplied token losses\" - that's exactly what we have. The \"stronger counterfactual\" is just the loss_without_call vs loss_with_call? Actually counterfactual: with call vs without. The subtraction gives gain. The \"normalized future-token weights\" likely means normalize by number of prediction tokens (future tokens). So that's our normalized gain. \"inclusive threshold\" means >= threshold. So we have the logic.\n\nNow, the solve(case) must raise ValueError for invalid input. Validate fields: threshold is number, calls is list, each call has required fields, prediction_tokens >0? But they say \"at least one prediction token\" for normalization. So if prediction_tokens is 0, it's invalid? Or we treat as 1? They said \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" That suggests if prediction_tokens is 0, we should use 1 to avoid division by zero. But also might be invalid. I'll assume we can use max(1, prediction_tokens) to avoid division by zero. But if prediction_tokens is 0, is that valid? Possibly they can be 0? In the fixtures, they are positive. For safety, I'll use max(1, prediction_tokens). But the instruction says \"validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check types and that prediction_tokens is int >=0? Could raise ValueError if prediction_tokens <=0? But they said \"at least one prediction token\" meaning denominator at least 1. So if prediction_tokens is 0, we could treat as invalid. I'll treat prediction_tokens must be positive integer (>=1). Otherwise raise ValueError.\n\nAlso parseable is boolean. threshold is float.\n\nReturn object: {\"accepted_call_ids\": [list of call_ids in order of original calls that pass], \"diagnostics\": [list of dicts for each call in order, e.g., {\"call_id\": ..., \"gain\": ..., \"normalized_gain\": ..., \"accepted\": ...} ]}. Gain and normalized_gain should be rounded. Rounded to how many decimals? Not specified; maybe we round to 8 decimal places or use round(x, 6). Since the input numbers have many decimals, we can round to 8. But the answer should be JSON. I'll round to 8 decimal places. Or we could just round to 6. I'll use 8.\n\nThe diagnostics \"rounded\" suggests we round the numbers. We'll round gain and normalized_gain.\n\nNow implement solve(case). It receives the payload directly (a dict). We must not mutate case. We'll extract threshold and calls, validate, compute for each call, build diagnostics per call, determine accepted list, and return.\n\nEdge cases: threshold could be negative? Possibly. We'll just compare.\n\nWe'll also handle missing fields? Raise ValueError.\n\nThe code should be Python standard library only.\n\nWe'll produce a single function definition.\n\nNow write the code as a string within JSON.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and quotes.\n\nPlan:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate payload structure\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be a dict\")\n    if \"threshold\" not in case or \"calls\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"missing threshold or calls\")\n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\n        raise ValueError(\"threshold must be numeric\")\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be a list\")\n    # validate each call\n    diag_list = []\n    accepted_ids = []\n    for i, call in enumerate(calls):\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\n            raise ValueError(f\"call {i} is not a dict\")\n        required = [\"call_id\", \"loss_without_call\", \"loss_with_call\", \"prediction_tokens\", \"parseable\"]\n        for key in required:\n            if key not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"call {i} missing {key}\")\n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        loss_without = call[\"loss_without_call\"]\n        loss_with = call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        pred_tokens = call[\"prediction_tokens\"]\n        parseable = call[\"parseable\"]\n        if not isinstance(call_id, str):\n            raise ValueError(f\"call {i} call_id not string\")\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)) or not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(f\"call {i} loss values must be numeric\")\n        if not isinstance(pred_tokens, int) or pred_tokens < 1:\n            raise ValueError(f\"call {i} prediction_tokens must be positive integer\")\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\n            raise ValueError(f\"call {i} parseable must be boolean\")\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        # normalize by prediction_tokens (at least 1, but pred_tokens is already >=1)\n        normalized_gain = gain / pred_tokens\n        accepted = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold\n        # round to 8 decimal places\n        gain_rounded = round(gain, 8)\n        normalized_rounded = round(normalized_gain, 8)\n        diag = {\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"gain\": gain_rounded,\n            \"normalized_gain\": normalized_rounded,\n            \"accepted\": accepted\n        }\n        diag_list.append(diag)\n        if accepted:\n            accepted_ids.append(call_id)\n    result = {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diag_list\n    }\n    return result\n\nBut the instruction says \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" It might expect the diagnostics object to be per call, but maybe only for accepted? It says \"per-call diagnostics\" and \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\" That suggests for all calls. So our output includes all.\n\nAlso note the phrase \"stronger_counterfactual\" - we didn't use it separately. The subtraction is the counterfactual comparison.\n\nWe also need to consider \"weighted losses\" - but there's no weighting given. So we just subtract.\n\nNow write the code as a Python source string.\n\nWe need to output JSON with key \"implementation\". The source code must be escaped. We'll produce the JSON object. Since the prompt says \"Your entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with the single key \"implementation\".\" So we output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ... \"}\n\nWe'll write the code with proper indentation and escaping.\n\nMake sure no markdown fences.\n\nNow produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1832,"completion_tokens":2771,"total_tokens":4603,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2142},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":0,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":1832},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}